[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Accepted (3...exf4) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/616eb960-51e3-48ed-858e-d28ed6a7753e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight. This is slightly different than 2.Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! We offer Black a capture of our f-pawn in order to push 4.e5 and attack the enemy knight...} 3.f4 3...exf4 {You will face this a shocking amount of times. It simply lands Black in a dead lost position on move 3! This is our plan: 4.e5 5.Nf3 6.d4 Finally, aim to capture the pawn back.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {Black decides to retreat the knight - this is the only safe square. Black is now threatening Qh4+. You MUST play Nf3 before you push d4, as this prevents Black's Qh4+.} 5.Nf3 5...d6 {Black undermines your pawn, and prepares to develop his light-squared bishop. We continue with 6.d4. If Black captures in the center afterwards, our queen will become active.} 6.d4 6...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We continue with 7.Qe2 - this is very important! Now Black is frozen, you avoid a trade of queens, and you will recapture both center pawns before castling - preferably queenside.} 7.Qe2 7...Nc6 {7...Nc6 is the most popular response at this point, but Black can do other things (we will review briefly in the video). We take on f4 with the bishop. This is the important move, taking advantage of the pin. We also set up traps.} 8.Bxf4 8...Nxd4 {Black takes, threatening your queen. We trade knights.} 9.Nxd4 9...Qxd4 {Black recaptures on d4.} 10.Bxe5 {Success! We capture Black's e-pawn and Black is going to lose everything.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Accepted (3...exf4) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/616eb960-51e3-48ed-858e-d28ed6a7753e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight. This is slightly different than 2.Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! We offer Black a capture of our f-pawn in order to push 4.e5 and attack the enemy knight...} 3.f4 3...exf4 {You will face this a shocking amount of times. It simply lands Black in a dead lost position on move 3! This is our plan: 4.e5 5.Nf3 6.d4 Finally, aim to capture the pawn back.} 4.e5 4...Qe7 {Black plays 4...Qe7 and pins the e-pawn. We continue by unpinning the pawn with 5.Qe2 - mirroring Black's move. Now, Black has no choice - the knight must depart.} 5.Qe2 5...Ng8 {Black's knight retreats. Black is now threatening Qh4+. We now develop and cover the check.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black might opt for 6...Nc6.} 7.d4 {We continue with 7.d4 and the threat of Nd5 is borderline unstoppable. White has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Accepted (3...exf4) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/616eb960-51e3-48ed-858e-d28ed6a7753e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight. This is slightly different than 2.Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! We offer Black a capture of our f-pawn in order to push 4.e5 and attack the enemy knight...} 3.f4 3...exf4 {You will face this a shocking amount of times. It simply lands Black in a dead lost position on move 3! This is our plan: 4.e5 5.Nf3 6.d4 Finally, aim to capture the pawn back.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {Black decides to retreat the knight - this is the only safe square. Black is now threatening Qh4+. You MUST play Nf3 before you push d4, as this prevents Black's Qh4+.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {Black can also continue with 5...Nc6, a more flexible move. We continue with d4 - expanding in the center and supporting the e-pawn.} 6.d4 6...d6 {Black undermines your pawn, and prepares to develop their light-squared Bishop. We capture Black's f4-pawn.} 7.Bxf4 7...dxe5 {Black captures our e-pawn.} 8.Qe2 {We continue with 8.Qe2. We know this position. 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 and we will play Bxe5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 1: The Gambit Accepted (3...exf4) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/616eb960-51e3-48ed-858e-d28ed6a7753e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight. This is slightly different than 2.Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit! We offer Black a capture of our f-pawn in order to push 4.e5 and attack the enemy knight...} 3.f4 3...exf4 {You will face this a shocking amount of times. It simply lands Black in a dead lost position on move 3! This is our plan: 4.e5 5.Nf3 6.d4 Finally, aim to capture the pawn back.} 4.e5 4...Qe7 {Black plays 4...Qe7 and pins the e-pawn. We continue by unpinning the pawn with 5.Qe2 - mirroring Black's move. Now, Black has no choice - the knight must depart.} 5.Qe2 5...Ng8 {Black's knight retreats. Black is now threatening Qh4+. We now develop and cover the check.} 6.Nf3 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6 to develop the light-squared Bishop. Let's play the knockout punch, punishing the queen for standing in front of the King.} 7.Nd5 7...Qd7 {Black's queen slides to safety, but...} 8.exd6+ {We chop down Black's position and win the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 2: Declining the Gambit (3...Nc6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/1b65ad65-731d-4bf7-90b2-396a05722146/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...Nc6 {Believe it or not, defending the Vienna Gambit with 3...Nc6 is one of Black's most popular responses. But, fortunately for us, this puts Black in a losing position! You must take after Black plays 3...Nc6.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. You have to push d4 to target the knights.} 5.d4 5...Nc6 {Of course, Black can go back this way too, but it's not quite effective. We push 6.e5, advancing and attacking the knight.} 6.e5 6...Qe7 {A tricky response for Black would be to pin our e-pawn with 6... Qe7. If we mirror the move with 7. Qe2 then Black can respond with 7... Nxd4.} 7.Be2 {Instead, we should simply respond with 7.Be2. This unpins our e-pawn, and keeps our queen watching our d-pawn. A reasonable move after this would be something like Nf3, defending the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 2: Declining the Gambit (3...Nc6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/1b65ad65-731d-4bf7-90b2-396a05722146/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...Nc6 {Believe it or not, defending the Vienna Gambit with 3...Nc6 is one of Black's most popular responses. But, fortunately for us, this puts Black in a losing position! You must take after Black plays 3...Nc6.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. You have to push d4 to target the knights.} 5.d4 5...Nc6 {Of course, Black can go back this way too, but it's not quite effective. We push 6.e5, advancing and attacking the knight.} 6.e5 6...Ng8 {Black retreats the knight to g8. We again cover Qh4+ and defend the center. 7.Nf3 Bb4 8.d5 is good for us.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {Black undermines the e-pawn, and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue by pinning Black's knight with 8.Bb5.} 8.Bb5 8...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We recapture with the knight, pressuring the c6-knight.} 9.Nxe5 9...Bd7 {Black supports the c6-knight with 9...Bd7. We will capture the bishop with 10.Nxd7.} 10.Nxd7 10...Qxd7 {Black recaptures on d7 and is ready to castle queenside. Let's make sure they don't get the time.} 11.d5 {We continue with 11.d5, creating tactics in our favor. 11...Qe7+ 12.Qe2 a6 13.dxc6 and White comes out on top because we can take on b7 at the end of many variations.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 2: Declining the Gambit (3...Nc6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/1b65ad65-731d-4bf7-90b2-396a05722146/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...Nc6 {Believe it or not, defending the Vienna Gambit with 3...Nc6 is one of Black's most popular responses. But, fortunately for us, this puts Black in a losing position! You must take after Black plays 3...Nc6.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. You have to push d4 to target the knights.} 5.d4 5...Ng6 {This is 1 of 2 retreats available for Black. Of the 2, this one is the better option for Black. We push 6.e5 and attack Black's knight! Black must retreat with ...Ng8 now.} 6.e5 6...Ng8 {Black retreats the knight to g8. Nf3 is a flexible developing move and it prevents an annoying Qh4+.} 7.Nf3 7...d6 {7...d6 is Black's most direct attempt at fighting back against the center. We don't need to be afraid - we develop the light-squared bishop and target Black's king.} 8.Bc4 8...dxe5 {Black captures the e-pawn. We simply castle. We now have the entire kingside developed, and we're ready to strike.} 9.O-O 9...exd4 {Black is greedy and captures the d-pawn.} 10.Ng5 {We continue with 10.Ng5. There are unstoppable intentions on f7. White is completely winning!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {Black develops with 4...Nc6, supporting the center - this is most common. We will pin the c6 knight to make the e-pawn a target.} 5.Bb5 5...Bd7 {Black unpins the knight with 5...Bd7. We continue with 6.d3. Now we are ready to capture on c6, followed by e5.} 6.d3 6...exf4 {Black releases the tension and captures on f4.} 7.Bxf4 {We recapture with the bishop. Our position is very comfortable and we will continue with Qd2 or Qe2, followed by castling kingside or queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {Black develops with 4...Nc6, supporting the center - this is most common. We will pin the c6 knight to make the e-pawn a target.} 5.Bb5 5...Bd7 {Black unpins the knight with 5...Bd7. We continue with 6.d3. Now we are ready to capture on c6, followed by e5.} 6.d3 6...Nd4 {Black also has the option to jump the knight onto d4. The following line is pretty forced. We capture the bishop on d7 with check.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen. We capture the knight on d4.} 8.Nxd4 8...exd4 {Black recaptures on d4 and attacks our knight. We retreat our knight to e2 and attack the d-pawn.} 9.Ne2 9...c5 {Black defends the pawn with 9...c5.} 10.O-O {We castle our king. We have a pretty comfortable position. If Black castles kingside some of our ideas include Ng3, Bd2 and f5. If Black castles queenside, some of our attacking ideas include a4 + b4 + Rb1, but we can also attack Black's center with c3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {Black develops with 4...Nc6, supporting the center - this is most common. We will pin the c6 knight to make the e-pawn a target.} 5.Bb5 5...Bg4 {Sometimes, this pin occurs. We should attack the bishop with 6.h3.} 6.h3 6...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxf3 7...Be7 {Black develops with 7...Be7 and prepares O-O. I recommend a very simple transformation of the position. We capture on c6 - giving up the bishop pair, but ruining Black's pawn structure.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We capture on e5.} 9.fxe5 9...dxe5 {Black recaptures and now their pawn structure is in ruins. We shall castle, push d3, and have a calm advantage.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black also castles their king.} 11.d3 {We continue 11.d3, with a pleasant position. We will develop the dark-squared bishop and attempt to target Black's weaknesses. If Black activates their rook with ...Rb8, don't play b3! Instead, we can support the pawn with Nd1 or Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black can also pin our knight with 4...Bg4 - this is actually the second most common move. We immediately attack the bishop with 5.h3.} 5.h3 5...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 6.Qxf3 6...Nc6 {Black develops with 6...Nc6, supporting the center. We continue with 7.Bb5.} 7.Bb5 {Our main objectives are to undermine e5. We will take on c6 and double the pawns, and then open up the f-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black can also pin our knight with 4...Bg4 - this is actually the second most common move. We immediately attack the bishop with 5.h3.} 5.h3 5...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 6.Qxf3 6...exf4 {If Black takes on f4, we can play d4! Bxf4 will follow, and White has a position with total central control. 0-0 will also add a rook to the semi-open f-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black can also pin our knight with 4...Bg4 - this is actually the second most common move. We immediately attack the bishop with 5.h3.} 5.h3 5...Bxf3 {Black captures on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 6.Qxf3 6...Be7 {If Black opts to develop with 6...Be7, we can continue developing with Bc4 + O-O + d3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black can also pin our knight with 4...Bg4 - this is actually the second most common move. We immediately attack the bishop with 5.h3.} 5.h3 5...Bh5 {If Black retreats the bishop to h5, we can trap it with g4 + f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...exf4 {Black captures on f4, but there is nothing to worry about here. We continue with 5.d4 - grabbing central space and opening up an attack on Black's f4-pawn.} 5.d4 5...Qe7 {Black plays 5...Qe7 - counterattacking our center. Don't rush, respect the opponent's threats.} 6.Bd3 {We defend the e-pawn with 6.Bd3. White is better, and we will simply continue by capturing on f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 3: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/6b99c806-2282-4000-8dff-3f1a6ec66dff/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image 1...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with 2...Nf6, allowing us to push 3.f4 next - the Vienna Gambit!} 3.f4 3...d6 {Declining with 3...d6 is extremely common. We will simply develop our knight.} 4.Nf3 4...exf4 {Black captures on f4, but there is nothing to worry about here. We continue with 5.d4 - grabbing central space and opening up an attack on Black's f4-pawn.} 5.d4 5...Nh5 {Black can also try to defend the pawn with 5...Nh5. We respond 6.Nd5 - attacking the f4 pawn once more.} 6.Nd5 6...g5 {Black defends the f-pawn with 6...g5, but this is a mistake! We sacrifice the knight on g5!} 7.Nxg5 7...Qxg5 {Black captures on g5. We can capture Black's knight on h5, temporarily sacrificing our queen!} 8.Qxh5 8...Qxh5 {Black recaptures on h5.} 9.Nf6+ {We continue with 9.Nf6+, forking Black's king and queen! Black's king is now forced to move. Soon we will capture the queen and win the f4-pawn. Black's position is terrible and White has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/8ad693dc-dffa-4d25-93b9-d474d18fafb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6! As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible. We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {This is the most popular move. Black develops a knight. 4.d3 protects the e4 pawn and opens up the dark-squared bishop. 5.f4 will be our next move.} 4.d3 4...Be7 {Black usually continues with normal development. Let's play f4!} 5.f4 5...O-O {Sometimes, Black just castles. Let's add pressure on e5 with our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black defends. And here we can go f5!} 7.f5 7...Na5 {Black usually goes for the bishop, as they should. If they don't do this, feel free to preserve your bishop with a3 ideas. And here I suggest we develop the other bishop.} 8.Bg5 8...Nxc4 {The bishop is taken...} 9.dxc4 {We recapture. We're going to bring our queen up and castle queenside to attack on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/8ad693dc-dffa-4d25-93b9-d474d18fafb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6! As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible. We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup.} 3.Bc4 3...f5 {Some players are crazy like this! They go for ...f5 to undermine the center. Let's just take. Did Black really think this through?} 4.exf5 4...Bxf5 {Black here usually recaptures. But now we have a plan: Nf3, 0-0 and d4!} 5.Nf3 5...Nf6 {Thus after Black develops, we simply continue with our plan of 0-0.} 6.O-O 6...Nc6 {Black develops the knight, and so here comes d4! This is actually very strong: if massive exchanges occur on e5, Black would have an isolated pawn!} 7.d4 7...exd4 {Just to exemplify how badly things can go, we can give a check here.} 8.Re1+ 8...Be7 {If the bishop blocks, Nd5 adds an attacker...} 9.Nd5 9...Nxd5 {Black is compelled to take, and logically we recapture.} 10.Bxd5 10...Kf8 {Black is advised to break the pin because Bg5 was coming! Let's remove d4's defender.} 11.Bxc6 11...bxc6 {And after Black takes...} 12.Nxd4 {The knight jumps into the center of the board! White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/8ad693dc-dffa-4d25-93b9-d474d18fafb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6! As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible. We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {This is the most popular move. Black develops a knight. 4.d3 protects the e4 pawn and opens up the dark-squared bishop. 5.f4 will be our next move.} 4.d3 4...Be7 {Black usually continues with normal development. Let's play f4!} 5.f4 5...exf4 {It's not unusual for Black to take. We just recapture, that's why our bishop is there!} 6.Bxf4 6...O-O {Black castles. And now we continue developing our pieces, getting ready to castle.} 7.Nf3 7...Bg4 {If Black goes for this very popular move, just kick them out with h3. If Bxf3, Qxf3, and you have the bishop pair.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {The bishop goes back, so we attack it once more.} 9.g4 9...Bg6 {It goes back. We now develop the queen.} 10.Qd2 {Now, in this particular position, 0-0-0 is more logical! The attack has been set up on the kingside!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/8ad693dc-dffa-4d25-93b9-d474d18fafb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6! As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible. We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup.} 3.Bc4 3...c6 {For some reason, this move is the second most popular on the lichess player database. d3 and f4 is our plan. Without a knight on c6, Black is less equipped to deal with pressure on e5.} 4.d3 4...Nf6 {The knight is brought out to support d5. We push.} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black resolves the tension before pushing d5. We recapture.} 6.Bxf4 6...d5 {...d5 is achieved, but we can simply trade.} 7.exd5 7...cxd5 {Black takes. Let's bring the bishop to b3.} 8.Bb3 8...d4 {And if d4, we first insert a check.} 9.Qe2+ 9...Be7 {And once the bishop blocks...} 10.Ne4 {We can get our knight out of danger and castle afterwards.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 4: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/8ad693dc-dffa-4d25-93b9-d474d18fafb3/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or d6! As 2...d6 is our subject, we'll see that in this study.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {This is definitely a passive setup, but it's completely possible. We develop the bishop first, before going for the d3/f4 setup.} 3.Bc4 3...c6 {For some reason, this move is the second most popular on the lichess player database. d3 and f4 is our plan. Without a knight on c6, Black is less equipped to deal with pressure on e5.} 4.d3 4...b5 {If Black goes for b5 ideas, we bring the bishop backwards.} 5.Bb3 5...a5 {And if they threaten to trap your bishop...} 6.a4 {Just don't let that happen! Our idea is to play f4 and Nf3. If 6...b4 7.Nce2 is solid for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {Black could develop their bishop to b4. An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex. Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4!} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black could also just take in the center right away, they often do this. We recapture, forking bishop and pawn.} 4.Qxd4 4...Bxc3+ {That's why many players just take. We recapture on c3.} 5.Qxc3 5...Nf6 {And again, we have a pawn in the center and Black has none. We can just develop our bishop to castle queenside soon. The knight can't take on e4 because we would just take on g7.} 6.Be3 6...Qe7 {So maybe now the queen can be developed to attack e4. We can defend with f3. Black's best plan would then be ...Nc6 + ...Qb4 for a queen trade.} 7.f3 7...Nc6 {So suppose they play Nc6 which is a developing move...} 8.O-O-O {Just castle queenside, and you'll stand much better. Even if Black does find the idea of trading queens, we can just go ahead and trade and develop our bishops which control many squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet. Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4. If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5.} 3.f4 3...d6 {Black could choose to defend the pawn. We develop the knight, attacking e5.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {4...Nf6 is also possible. We'll just follow our standard Vienna strategy: Bc4 and d3.} 5.Bc4 5...O-O {Black castles. And d3 opens up our c1 bishop and connects our pawns.} 6.d3 6...Nc6 {6...Nc6 is a close second in terms of popularity. I suggest we prioritize kingside play with f5. Bg5 is a strong idea.} 7.f5 7...Nd4 {Black's best move is ...Nd4. We should put our knight on d5.} 8.Nd5 8...Nxd5 {If Black takes, we've got to keep the integrity of our structure by recapturing with the bishop.} 9.Bxd5 9...c6 {Black usually pushes the pawn to dislodge our bishop...} 10.Bb3 {In which case we should just retreat. In case of ...a5, we've got Nxd4 and a4, followed by Qf3 to develop our queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet. Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4. If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5.} 3.f4 3...d6 {Black could choose to defend the pawn. We develop the knight, attacking e5.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {4...Nf6 is also possible. We'll just follow our standard Vienna strategy: Bc4 and d3.} 5.Bc4 5...O-O {Black castles. And d3 opens up our c1 bishop and connects our pawns.} 6.d3 6...Ng4 {Probably the scariest-looking move is 6...Ng4. We should just get out of the fork because Black's minor piece expedition doesn't achieve much. We can play Qe2 and even if we lose castling rights, Black will have to waste too much time.} 7.Qe2 7...Bf2+ {Check! But we can just come into f1 and Black is forced to move the bishop again.} 8.Kf1 8...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most natural move because h3 was a threat. But after 9.f5, White is much better. It's hard to suggest a move for Black.} 9.f5 9...Nc6 {If 9...Nc6, 10.Bg5 is also great for White. We will play Bg5 because Black has a problem on the kingside. They lack defenders.} 10.Bg5 10...Nd4 {But if this happens...} 11.Qd2 {You can keep all the threats alive by moving the queen away. White has a winning position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet. Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4. If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5.} 3.f4 3...d6 {Black could choose to defend the pawn. We develop the knight, attacking e5.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {Black responds by defending with the knight. Let's just go for the bishop with Na4.} 5.Na4 5...Bb6 {The bishop goes back. Now we get the bishop pair.} 6.Nxb6 6...axb6 {Black recaptures. We pin the knight, thus increasing pressure on e5.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {Black breaks the pin and now defends the knight. If we capture on c6, they'll recapture with the bishop and they'll be attacking e4. That's why we should defend e4 here.} 8.d3 8...Nge7 {If Black just develops their knight...} 9.f5 {We can push with f5 and Black should expect an attack from us on the kingside!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {Black could develop their bishop to b4. An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex. Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4!} 3.d4 3...Nc6 {The simple 3...Nc6 is also not optimal. We can just take in the center. Now if Black throws in Bxc3+, we'll just have the bishop pair in an open position.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe5 {The most popular move is recapturing. So now we can fork both pieces.} 5.Qd4 5...Bxc3+ {And now after Black captures, we recapture with the queen, with pressure on the knight.} 6.Qxc3 6...d6 {And after these very natural moves, White has an advantage. We can just develop and attack e5.} 7.Nf3 7...Nxf3+ {And let's say Black chooses the very natural trade here...} 8.gxf3 {Who's got the stronger center and the better prospect for their pieces? Certainly White. We have the bishop pair advantage and pressure on the g7 and c7 pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet. Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4. If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5.} 3.f4 3...d6 {Black could choose to defend the pawn. We develop the knight, attacking e5.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {4...Nf6 is also possible. We'll just follow our standard Vienna strategy: Bc4 and d3.} 5.Bc4 5...O-O {Black castles. And d3 opens up our c1 bishop and connects our pawns.} 6.d3 6...Bg4 {6...Bg4 is the most popular move. Let's now try to achieve castling. To do that, we'll have to eliminate Black's dark-squared bishop with Na4.} 7.Na4 7...Bb6 {Bringing the bishop to b6 is the most popular move. Let's just take to remove Black's influence on the diagonal.} 8.Nxb6 8...axb6 {Black captures towards the center.} 9.O-O {And we achieve 0-0. We'll go c3 and Bxf4 if Black takes on f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {Black can choose to develop the bishop first, without committing the knight yet. Here I suggest we go for the immediate f4. If Black takes, you'll notice there's no knight on f6 to harass with e5.} 3.f4 3...exf4 {So if Black takes, I advise you to develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...d6 {Passive continuations, which are popular, can be taken advantage of.. We can attack the bishop and open up an attack on the pawn.} 5.d4 5...Bb6 {The bishop goes back. And we recapture our pawn and enjoy of greater central space.} 6.Bxf4 6...Bg4 {Many players like to develop and pin the knight to pressure d4. But we can pressure Black's bishop off the diagonal. What's the point? The point is that if Black goes for the variation where they win the d4 pawn, they'll be too down in development.} 7.h3 7...Bxf3 {Black takes, and we recapture.} 8.Qxf3 8...Bxd4 {Black wins a pawn!} 9.O-O-O {And we castle queenside, enjoying a strong lead in development and threatening e5 to open up the position!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {Black could develop their bishop to b4. An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex. Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4!} 3.d4 3...d6 {...d6, defending e5 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain, is also natural. We will simply trade pawns on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures. Now we trade queens and make Black lose castling rights.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {They recapture. We defend our knight.} 6.Bd2 6...Nf6 {Black adds pressure on e4. But we can castle and defend e4 indirectly.} 7.O-O-O 7...Ke7 {The king gets out of the way. And now we defend e4 with our rook.} 8.Re1 8...Rd8 {If the black rook enters the open d-file, we can actually get the bishop out of the diagonal starting with a3.} 9.a3 9...Bd6 {And once they move up...} 10.f4 {f4 is quite strong, and puts pressure on the Black position! If 10...exf4, 11.e5! We've got sound developing moves like Nf3 and Bc4 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {Black could develop their bishop to b4. An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex. Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4!} 3.d4 3...d6 {...d6, defending e5 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain, is also natural. We will simply trade pawns on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...Bxc3+ {If Black chooses to interject this move, we can just recapture. Now we have the bishop pair.} 5.bxc3 5...dxe5 {Black takes in the center. We trade queens.} 6.Qxd8+ 6...Kxd8 {Black recaptures. We play Bc4. Things can go wrong for Black quickly.} 7.Bc4 7...f6 {They can defend, but our bishops are coming to life. This is the bishop pair advantage! We open up lines with f4.} 8.f4 8...exf4 {If they take, sure they have a better structure.} 9.Bxf4 {But our piece activity is just insane. White is much better. We'll play 0-0-0+, Nf3, and our idea will be to get the e5-pawn push to get rid of our isolated e-pawn. If Black ever plays ...Bg4, h3 will force it off the diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Vienna Gambit (Accepted & Declined) / Study 5: Move 2 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/1d44f3cc-2db6-47c2-a7c3-3030458355eb/studies/7578c8e0-998a-4d54-a285-89ec43b348f0/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black will mirror us. We'll play Nc3, starting up our Vienna. And now this is where Black will deviate. 2...Bc5, Bb4 or like in the last study, d6!} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {Black could develop their bishop to b4. An idea could be to trade off the bishop before playing d6 and going for a dark square color complex. Let's just expand in the center and play 3.d4!} 3.d4 3...Bxc3+ {Taking is very natural for Black. We can just recapture.} 4.bxc3 4...exd4 {Now Black takes in the center once again. But this just leaves us with a strong center!} 5.cxd4 5...d6 {d6 comes a little too late now, after Black has improved our position with all those bad trades. We can just develop normally and stand much better.} 6.Bd3 6...Ne7 {For example, Black develops, and we develop our knight, getting ready to castle.} 7.Ne2 7...O-O {Black castles...} 8.O-O {And so do we. Black needs counterplay with c5 fast, in which case we'll just go c3. Our center is really strong.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Main Line 3...d5 with 5...Nxc3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/21776690-b1c2-4c8c-9393-49a6e7ecc7eb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image ...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f4-push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with ...Nf6, allowing us to push f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop. After f4, this is the starting position of the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the pawn is far more common, or defending the center with ...d6 or ...Nc6. Black's counterattack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3), but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc3 {Black taking on c3 is the most common move by far. We will recapture with the b-pawn. This is the plan: d2-d4 + Bd3 + Ne2. Now Black has many options, but I will show you the universal setup and plans.} 6.bxc3 6...Nc6 {This variation is also popular, although not that good for Black. We will achieve d4 with a strong pawn chain.} 7.d4 7...Be7 {Black could also choose to go to the kingside. Here we just point our bishop at h7.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Black castles. And we develop our knight.} 9.Ne2 9...Bg5 {The position is simply uncomfortable for Black.} 10.O-O {White is already +3 according to the engine! It is easy for our minor pieces to focus on attacking the enemy king, and difficult for Black to get theirs across to defend.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Main Line 3...d5 with 5...Nxc3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/21776690-b1c2-4c8c-9393-49a6e7ecc7eb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image ...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f4-push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with ...Nf6, allowing us to push f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop. After f4, this is the starting position of the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the pawn is far more common, or defending the center with ...d6 or ...Nc6. Black's counterattack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3), but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc3 {Black taking on c3 is the most common move by far. We will recapture with the b-pawn. This is the plan: d2-d4 + Bd3 + Ne2. Now Black has many options, but I will show you the universal setup and plans.} 6.bxc3 6...c5 {This is Black's second approach, with 2 pawns in the center. We'll slide the queen to g3, keeping an eye on the g7-pawn and not allowing the dark-squared bishop to develop easily.} 7.Qg3 7...Nc6 {Black continues normal development, further increasing their central control. We continue by developing our knight to f3.} 8.Nf3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop to e6. We develop the light-squared bishop and pin Black's knight. We are ready to castle and apply pressure on the f-file and the b-file.} 9.Bb5 9...a6 {Black attacks our bishop with a7-a6 and forces a decision. We will capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but ruining Black's structure.} 10.Bxc6+ 10...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We'll jump to g5! This puts pressure on some vulnerable spots on the light squares, and prepares castling so that the rook activates on f1.} 11.Ng5 11...h6 {Black could make the mistake of attacking our knight with ...h6. We'll capture the bishop on e6.} 12.Nxe6 12...fxe6 {Black recaptures on e6. The diagonal to Black's king has now been weakened. Oops! We play Qg6+ and force Black's king to move.} 13.Qg6+ 13...Kd7 {Black moves the king to d7.} 14.O-O {We simply castle our king and we are crushing.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Main Line 3...d5 with 5...Nxc3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/21776690-b1c2-4c8c-9393-49a6e7ecc7eb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image ...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f4-push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with ...Nf6, allowing us to push f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop. After f4, this is the starting position of the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the pawn is far more common, or defending the center with ...d6 or ...Nc6. Black's counterattack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3), but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc3 {Black taking on c3 is the most common move by far. We will recapture with the b-pawn. This is the plan: d2-d4 + Bd3 + Ne2. Now Black has many options, but I will show you the universal setup and plans.} 6.bxc3 6...Nc6 {This variation is also popular, although not that good for Black. We will achieve d4 with a strong pawn chain.} 7.d4 7...Be6 {It's hard to suggest moves for Black. ...Be6 to play ...Qd7 and castle queenside is an option.} 8.Rb1 {But honestly I consider Rb1 refutes that idea. If Black wants to go Qd7, they'll lose the b-pawn! The rest of our development stays the same: Bd3 and Ne2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Main Line 3...d5 with 5...Nxc3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/21776690-b1c2-4c8c-9393-49a6e7ecc7eb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image ...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f4-push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with ...Nf6, allowing us to push f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop. After f4, this is the starting position of the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the pawn is far more common, or defending the center with ...d6 or ...Nc6. Black's counterattack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3), but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc3 {Black taking on c3 is the most common move by far. We will recapture with the b-pawn. This is the plan: d2-d4 + Bd3 + Ne2. Now Black has many options, but I will show you the universal setup and plans.} 6.bxc3 6...Be7 {Black continues developing naturally and prepares to castle. We'll support the e5-pawn with d4.} 7.d4 7...O-O {Black continues naturally by castling their king. We develop our light-squared bishop to d3.} 8.Bd3 8...Be6 {Black develops their light-squared bishop to e6. We continue with our plan and develop with the knight to e2.} 9.Ne2 9...Nc6 {We have followed a relatively normal set up of moves for both sides, but actually, Black is completely lost! Since Black did not play ...c5, castle queenside, or fight back at all, we can bulldoze on the kingside!} 10.Nf4 {We jump in with Nf4! White is already completely winning. We will continue with O-O and Qh5 and Black will desperately struggle to defend against our raging attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Main Line 3...d5 with 5...Nxc3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/21776690-b1c2-4c8c-9393-49a6e7ecc7eb/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image ...e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f4-push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with ...Nf6, allowing us to push f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop. After f4, this is the starting position of the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the pawn is far more common, or defending the center with ...d6 or ...Nc6. Black's counterattack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3), but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc3 {Black taking on c3 is the most common move by far. We will recapture with the b-pawn. This is the plan: d2-d4 + Bd3 + Ne2. Now Black has many options, but I will show you the universal setup and plans.} 6.bxc3 6...Be7 {Black continues developing naturally and prepares to castle. We'll support the e5-pawn with d4.} 7.d4 7...O-O {Black continues naturally by castling their king. We develop our light-squared bishop to d3.} 8.Bd3 8...c5 {The approach of castling and pushing ...c5 is fine. We'll follow the same plan with Ne2, castling, and attacking black on the kingside.} 9.Ne2 9...c4 {...c4 is often played to attack the bishop. Let's propose a trade.} 10.Bf5 10...Bxf5 {Black accepts. We recapture.} 11.Qxf5 11...Qd7 {Queen trade?} 12.g4 {Sure! Our pawns will be impressive on the 5th rank!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...a6 {This is also a decent option for Black. Basically, Black is inviting the capture on c6 and trying to argue that they will have sufficient compensation in the form of the bishop pair. We capture on c6 - giving Black the bishop pair but ruining their pawn structure.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We will try to prepare castling with Ne2. Black is still fine with accurate play, though natural development here can quickly backfire.} 9.Ne2 9...Qh4+ {This is again Black's critical idea - using the fact that the e1-h4 diagonal was weakened on move 3. We have to block the check. Ng3 achieves this. While g3 is slightly inferior to Ng3, it is notable that WGM Carissa Yip managed to score a win with it against FM Yahli Sokolovsky in 2021.} 10.Ng3 10...Qg4 {Black is offering a queen trade and attempting to simultaneously develop the c8 bishop. We should simply decline the queen trade and proceed with our plan.} 11.Qf2 {We slide the queen back a square - declining the queen trade. Our plan is now to play O-O and develop our bishop to the e3 square from where it can go to d4 or even offer to trade itself on c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...Qh4+ {The critical response. Black is immediately trying to expose the e1-h4 diagonal which was weakened when White played f2-f4. Let's block it.} 8.g3 8...Qe4+ {Black gives a check and basically forces a queen trade. We will block with the bishop, developing.} 9.Be3 9...Qxc2 {Black takes on c2. We play Ne2 and prepare for castling. Black now has to come back and offer the trade again, as taking on b2 would lead to us castling with a huge attack. After 10.Ne2 Qxb2 11.0-0 we would attack f7!} 10.Ne2 10...Qe4 {Black offers the queen trade once again. This time we exchange the queens on e4.} 11.Qxe4 11...dxe4 {Black recaptures on e4. We will centralize our knight, adding an attacker on c6.} 12.Nd4 12...Bd7 {Black defends the knight. We capture on c6 - forcing Black to ruin their pawn structure.} 13.Nxc6 13...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 14.Ba4 {Down a pawn for the moment but with the amount of weaknesses (c6, c7, e4, f7) we'll continue pressure throughout the middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...Qh4+ {The critical response. Black is immediately trying to expose the e1-h4 diagonal which was weakened when White played f2-f4. Let's block it.} 8.g3 8...Qe4+ {Black gives a check and basically forces a queen trade. We will block with the bishop, developing.} 9.Be3 9...Qxe5 {Black gets greedy and captures on e5. We quickly castle queenside and try to obtain an initiative.} 10.O-O-O 10...Be6 {Black develops the bishop naturally and supports the center.} 11.Ne2 {We've sacrificed a pawn for an initiative. Our ideas include Nd4/f4, or Bf4, and moving the h1 rook to f1 or e1.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...Qh4+ {The critical response. Black is immediately trying to expose the e1-h4 diagonal which was weakened when White played f2-f4. Let's block it.} 8.g3 8...Qe4+ {Black gives a check and basically forces a queen trade. We will block with the bishop, developing.} 9.Be3 9...Qxe5 {Black gets greedy and captures on e5. We quickly castle queenside and try to obtain an initiative.} 10.O-O-O 10...Bg4 {This is a very resourceful tactical idea! We capture the "sacrificed" bishop as to not lose the d1 rook. However, this move undefends the e3 bishop.} 11.Qxg4 11...Qxe3+ {Black captures our bishop. We move out of check.} 12.Kb1 12...Qe4 {Black again offers the queen trade. We should definitely decline Black's offer since trading the queens would leave us down a pawn in a queenless middlegame.} 13.Qg5 {We decline the queen trade and attack the d5 pawn which cannot be conveniently defended. The position is objectively equal, although Black is the one that has to find the critical moves. It is also very important to note that Black can't really capture the h1 rook since Re1+ would lead to a devastating attack on Black's king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...Qh4+ {The critical response. Black is immediately trying to expose the e1-h4 diagonal which was weakened when White played f2-f4. Let's block it.} 8.g3 8...Qe4+ {Black gives a check and basically forces a queen trade. We will block with the bishop, developing.} 9.Be3 9...Qxf3 {This is basically what we wanted when we played Be3 - Black has traded queens on our terms and helped us develop quickly.} 10.Nxf3 {We will castle queenside, place our h1 rook on e1/f1 and attempt to put pressure on Black's d5 and f7 pawns. An option Black has now is 10...Bg4, but it can be comfortably met with Nd4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This is the simplest and most natural response. In this case we will actually capture away from the center with the d-pawn. This opens up the c1 bishop and allows us to further accelerate our development.} 7.dxc3 7...a6 {This is also a decent option for Black. Basically, Black is inviting the capture on c6 and trying to argue that they will have sufficient compensation in the form of the bishop pair. We capture on c6 - giving Black the bishop pair but ruining their pawn structure.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We will try to prepare castling with Ne2. Black is still fine with accurate play, though natural development here can quickly backfire.} 9.Ne2 9...Bc5 {Black develops naturally. Qg3 is an important move! We slide our queen over to g3 and attack the g7 pawn.} 10.Qg3 10...Rg8 {Black defends the g7 pawn.} 11.Bg5 {Our lead in development is now quite apparent and Black will still need a couple of moves to attempt to consolidate his position. White has a very comfortable advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/ef058380-3ee9-4954-9041-d91b883e8db1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push. This is slightly different than Nf3, which is standard development.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Capturing the f4-pawn is far more common, or defending the center with d7-d6 or Nb8-c6. Let's take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision. Our move is not the most common (5.Nf3 or 5.d3) but it is the most aggressive.} 5.Qf3 5...Nc6 {A tricky and challenging move. ...Nc6 threatens Nxe5, so we should prevent that. Let's go for a pin! We do not want to take on e4 yet.} 6.Bb5 6...f5 {Combining Nc6 with f5 doesn't really make much sense. Black's knight on e4 is pretty annoying, so we should immediately remove it with d3.} 7.d3 7...Nxc3 {Black is forced to make this capture as both 7...Nc5 & 7...Ng5 would allow the d pawn to be captured. We recapture on c3.} 8.bxc3 8...Be6 {A natural move - developing the bishop and solidifying the center.} 9.Ne2 {Our plan is now quite simple. We will O-O, support the e5 pawn with d4 and place our knight onto the f4 square where it will be exerting a great amount of pressure on Black's cramped position. Computers already suggest that White is borderline winning with a +2 advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 3: Black Overprotects With 5...f5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/1b40b74a-096a-41f8-89c6-03d375440819/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. We take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. By playing ...f5 Black has made a similar commitment that White made on move 3 - increasing their control of the center, but weakening the diagonal leading to their king. Let's attack the knight with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...d4 {This is the critical response - Black is "physically" preventing us from getting a solid central structure with c3-d4-e5. Now, it is important that we don't just capture this pawn as after ...Qxd4, both our rook and e5 pawn would be under attack. Let's move the queen to a more active square.} 8.Qg3 8...Nc6 {Black continues normal development - further increasing their central control. Another important moment: we must develop Be2 before moving our knight, as we threaten Bh5+ here.} 9.Be2 9...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6. This move also defends against the threat of Bh5+ as ...g6 is now a viable option since Bxg6+ can simply be met with ...hxg6 Qxg6+ Bf7. We will control d5 by pushing c4.} 10.c4 10...Qd7 {Black moves the queen up to d7 and prepares to castle queenside. We respond by moving our rook to b1 and attacking Black's b7-pawn. If Black ever gives a check with their bishop, we can move the king to d1, where it will be safe.} 11.Rb1 11...O-O-O {Black castles queenside and defends the b7-pawn with their king.} 12.Bf3 {We move our bishop to f3. With the opposite side castling, the position is incredibly dynamic and there are attacking prospects for both sides. Before trying to launch any sort of attack, we should look to finish our development with Ne2 and O-O. Any h7-h6 (looking to expand with g5) should simply be met with h2-h4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 3: Black Overprotects With 5...f5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/1b40b74a-096a-41f8-89c6-03d375440819/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. We take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. By playing ...f5 Black has made a similar commitment that White made on move 3 - increasing their control of the center, but weakening the diagonal leading to their king. Let's attack the knight with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...d4 {This is the critical response - Black is "physically" preventing us from getting a solid central structure with c3-d4-e5. Now, it is important that we don't just capture this pawn as after ...Qxd4, both our rook and e5 pawn would be under attack. Let's move the queen to a more active square.} 8.Qg3 8...Nc6 {Black continues normal development - further increasing their central control. Another important moment: we must develop Be2 before moving our knight, as we threaten Bh5+ here.} 9.Be2 9...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6. This move also defends against the threat of Bh5+ as ...g6 is now a viable option since Bxg6+ can simply be met with ...hxg6 Qxg6+ Bf7. We will control d5 by pushing c4.} 10.c4 10...Nb4 {Black jumps the knight into b4 and threatens to capture on c2.} 11.Bd1 {We defend the c2-pawn by retreating our bishop back to d1. The position is somewhat complicated, but White has an edge and White's position is easier to play - We will look to continue with Nh3 & O-O, and can at any point kick out Black's knight with a2-a3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 3: Black Overprotects With 5...f5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/1b40b74a-096a-41f8-89c6-03d375440819/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. We take on e5.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. By playing ...f5 Black has made a similar commitment that White made on move 3 - increasing their control of the center, but weakening the diagonal leading to their king. Let's attack the knight with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...d4 {This is the critical response - Black is "physically" preventing us from getting a solid central structure with c3-d4-e5. Now, it is important that we don't just capture this pawn as after ...Qxd4, both our rook and e5 pawn would be under attack. Let's move the queen to a more active square.} 8.Qg3 8...Nc6 {Black continues normal development - further increasing their central control. Another important moment: we must develop Be2 before moving our knight, as we threaten Bh5+ here.} 9.Be2 9...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6. This move also defends against the threat of Bh5+ as ...g6 is now a viable option since Bxg6+ can simply be met with ...hxg6 Qxg6+ Bf7. We will control d5 by pushing c4.} 10.c4 10...Bb4+ {Black makes use of the newly opened e1-a5 diagonal by moving their bishop to b4 and giving a check to our king. d1 is safe for it.} 11.Kd1 11...O-O {Black castles their king.} 12.Nh3 {We move our knight to h3. This move keeps the f3 square available for our light-squared bishop, and allows us to put our bishop/knight on g5. The computer gives equality, but White is very active and has good attacking prospects on the kingside. We can continue developing with Bg5/Ng5, Bf3 and Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 4: Sidelines After 5...f5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/a799272f-422e-495f-8925-f737219fdbc7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Black's counter-attack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. Black's knight on e4 is pretty annoying, so we should immediately remove it with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...c5 {This is inferior to the critical d5-d4, but is a very playable move and scores quite well for Black in the database. By playing c7-c5, Black has increased their control over the d4 square and is intending to develop their b8 knight behind the c-pawn. White can respond to 7...c5 in a couple of ways, but 8.g3 is a simple, yet quite an interesting idea.} 8.g3 8...Nc6 {Black simply continues by developing their knight, putting some pressure on the e5-pawn. We can ignore the attack and continue with our plan of Bg2, since ...Nxe5 can be met with Qe2 + d4. Black's d5-pawn would also be weak in many lines.} 9.Bg2 9...d4 {This somewhat counter-intuitive move is actually the critical response to ...Bg2. For the cost of the opening of the long diagonal, Black is successfully preventing us from getting a solid central structure with c3-d4-e5. Let's develop our knight, as development is more important than the e5-pawn.} 10.Ne2 10...Nxe5 {In this instance, Black does capture the undefended e5-pawn, as White no longer has the idea of Qe2 + d4. This is an important move - we should absolutely offer the queen trade on d5.} 11.Qd5 11...Qxd5 {Black trades the queens - there isn't really a better option since the e5 knight was attacked by White's queen.} 12.Bxd5 {We recapture on d5. Temporarily down a pawn, but with an advantage in development and sufficient dynamic compensation. Our compensation is, in fact, so sufficient that the engine is even suggesting Black gives up the b7-pawn with ...Bd7.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 4: Sidelines After 5...f5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/a799272f-422e-495f-8925-f737219fdbc7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Black's counter-attack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. Black's knight on e4 is pretty annoying, so we should immediately remove it with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...c5 {This is inferior to the critical d5-d4, but is a very playable move and scores quite well for Black in the database. By playing c7-c5, Black has increased their control over the d4 square and is intending to develop their b8 knight behind the c-pawn. White can respond to 7...c5 in a couple of ways, but 8.g3 is a simple, yet quite an interesting idea.} 8.g3 8...Nc6 {Black simply continues by developing their knight, putting some pressure on the e5-pawn. We can ignore the attack and continue with our plan of Bg2, since ...Nxe5 can be met with Qe2 + d4. Black's d5-pawn would also be weak in many lines.} 9.Bg2 9...Be6 {Black continues developing naturally and supports the d5 pawn. Nh3 is an important move! We want to bring the knight to g5, from where it will be exerting a great amount of pressure on Black's position.} 10.Nh3 10...Qd7 {Black develops the queen and prepares O-O-O.} 11.Ng5 {Our position is now amazing. The g5 knight is great and can capture the e6 bishop at any point - destabilizing Black's center. We are putting a large amount of pressure on the d5 pawn and can keep developing with O-O + Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 4: Sidelines After 5...f5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/a799272f-422e-495f-8925-f737219fdbc7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Black's counter-attack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. Black's knight on e4 is pretty annoying, so we should immediately remove it with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...Be6 {This is another natural developing move and is about as common as ...Be7. Again, Black didn't play the critical ...d4 so we should be able to obtain a pleasant position. We simply develop our knight to e2 - the same way we did it against 7...Be7.} 8.Ne2 8...Nc6 {This is probably most natural. Black develops the knight, attacking our e5 pawn and supporting the center. We can actually ignore the attack on the pawn and instead gain a tempo on Black's undefended e6 bishop.} 9.Nf4 9...Qd7 {Black defends the bishop and prepares O-O-O. 9...Nxe5 runs into 10.Qe2.} 10.d4 {We play d4 - defending our e5 pawn and establishing a very solid central structure. White's position is now very pleasant and we will continue to develop naturally with Bd3 + O-O + Rb1.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Vienna Gambit Best Line (3...d5) / Study 4: Sidelines After 5...f5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fc56408c-da12-474f-9f21-cd64f2b12156/studies/a799272f-422e-495f-8925-f737219fdbc7/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nf6, allowing us to push f2-f4 into the Vienna Gambit. Black can also develop the other knight or dark squared bishop.} 3.f4 3...d5 {And declining the Gambit is the best response for Black. However, at levels below 1600, this move is played roughly 10% of the time. Black's counter-attack leaves White with few options. We are pretty much forced to make this capture.} 4.fxe5 4...Nxe4 {As the Black knight is under attack, the natural move is 4...Nxe4. 5.Qf3 attacks the Black knight, forcing a decision.} 5.Qf3 5...f5 {I'll be very clear: this is the best move in my experience, but we will try to mix things up here regardless. Black's knight on e4 is pretty annoying, so we should immediately remove it with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nxc3 {This is Black's simplest & most natural response to the attack on their knight. We recapture on c3. Now if Black doesn't play the critical d5-d4, we will get a very easy game.} 7.bxc3 7...Be7 {Black doesn't play the critical 7...d4 and develops naturally. We simply develop our knight to e2 - from here it supports the d4 square and has access to the f4 square where it is likely to land in the future.} 8.Ne2 8...O-O {Black keeps developing naturally.} 9.g3 {We play g3 and will fianchetto our bishop on g2. The position is quite pleasant for White, and our plan now consists of Bg2 + d4 + O-O in some order.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 1: The Queen Defends (4...Qf6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/78d5a2d2-522a-41c7-b7a1-95f978783a78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We simply develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. This variation is extremely common and has been played in over 120 master games and 320,000 games, but it's a mistake because of 4.Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...Qf6 {4...Qf6 is by far the most common, but also the worst of the three options. A natural move to attack and defend. But as it turns out, after ...Qf6 Black is already borderline lost! We have the stunning idea - Nd5! We attack Black's queen and threaten to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7, but we also shockingly allow Black to capture on f2.} 5.Nd5 5...Qxf2+ {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture as every other move loses material - either to Nxc7+ or Qxg7. We slide our king over to d1, the only legal move. Now, our king is in no real danger, and we have multiple threats: Qxg7, Nxc7+ and even Nh3! Black will try to defend in two ways: 6...Kf8 and 6...g6.} 6.Kd1 6...g6 {Black defends the g7 pawn with g7-g6. Now Nxc7+ might look really tempting, but that is not the point. The point is Nh3! - and the queen is actually trapped.} 7.Nh3 7...Qd4 {Black retreats the queen to the only "free" square where it cannot be immediately captured and attacks the c4 bishop. We defend the bishop with d2-d3 and threaten to trap Black's queen with c2-c3.} 8.d3 8...Bb6 {Noticing that their queen is getting trapped, Black attempts to free up the c5 square for their queen. We move our rook to the semi-open file and shift our focus from trapping Black's queen to attacking Black on the king-side.} 9.Rf1 9...d6 {Black plays d7-d6 and opens up an attack on our queen.} 10.Qf3 {We simply slide the Queen back to f3 - out of the vision of Black's bishop. Our attacking possibilites are now endless, Black's pieces are terrible & have no harmony, and White is just completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 1: The Queen Defends (4...Qf6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/78d5a2d2-522a-41c7-b7a1-95f978783a78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We simply develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. This variation is extremely common and has been played in over 120 master games and 320,000 games, but it's a mistake because of 4.Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...Qf6 {4...Qf6 is by far the most common, but also the worst of the three options. A natural move to attack and defend. But as it turns out, after ...Qf6 Black is already borderline lost! We have the stunning idea - Nd5! We attack Black's queen and threaten to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7, but we also shockingly allow Black to capture on f2.} 5.Nd5 5...Qxf2+ {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture as every other move loses material - either to Nxc7+ or Qxg7. We slide our king over to d1, the only legal move. Now, our king is in no real danger, and we have multiple threats: Qxg7, Nxc7+ and even Nh3! Black will try to defend in two ways: 6...Kf8 and 6...g6.} 6.Kd1 6...g6 {Black defends the g7 pawn with g7-g6. Now Nxc7+ might look really tempting, but that is not the point. The point is Nh3! - and the queen is actually trapped.} 7.Nh3 7...Qd4 {Black retreats the queen to the only "free" square where it cannot be immediately captured and attacks the c4 bishop. We defend the bishop with d2-d3 and threaten to trap Black's queen with c2-c3.} 8.d3 8...d6 {Black plays d7-d6 and opens up an attack on our queen.} 9.Qf3 {We simply slide the Queen back to f3 - out of the vision of Black's bishop. The threat of c2-c3 is now simply unstoppable and White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 1: The Queen Defends (4...Qf6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/78d5a2d2-522a-41c7-b7a1-95f978783a78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We simply develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. This variation is extremely common and has been played in over 120 master games and 320,000 games, but it's a mistake because of 4.Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...Qf6 {4...Qf6 is by far the most common, but also the worst of the three options. A natural move to attack and defend. But as it turns out, after ...Qf6 Black is already borderline lost! We have the stunning idea - Nd5! We attack Black's queen and threaten to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7, but we also shockingly allow Black to capture on f2.} 5.Nd5 5...Qxf2+ {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture as every other move loses material - either to Nxc7+ or Qxg7. We slide our king over to d1, the only legal move. Now, our king is in no real danger, and we have multiple threats: Qxg7, Nxc7+ and even Nh3! Black will try to defend in two ways: 6...Kf8 and 6...g6.} 6.Kd1 6...Kf8 {Black defends the g7 pawn with the king and simultaneously moves out of the way of Nxc7+. However, Black is not out of the woods whatsoever. We attack the queen with Nh3! As it turns out, Black's queen doesn't really have any squares to move to other than d4, where it is going to get into a lot of trouble.} 7.Nh3 7...Qd4 {Black moves the queen to the only "safe" square and attacks the c4 bishop. We defend the bishop with d2-d3. Black's queen is suddenly starting to feel quite stuck, and our main threat would be c2-c3, which would trap Black's queen in the center of the board.} 8.d3 8...d6 {Black plays d7-d6 and opens up an attack on our queen. We simply move the queen to f3 - out of the vision of Black's bishop & still keeping the threat of c2-c3.} 9.Qf3 9...Bxh3 {Most players will now play ...Bxh3, and we should not rush to recapture on h3. Instead, we throw in Rf1 and threaten checkmate on f7.} 10.Rf1 10...Be6 {Black retreats the bishop and defends the f7 square.} 11.c3 {We finally get to play our idea: c2-c3. Black's queen is now trapped and White is completely winning with a major material advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 1: The Queen Defends (4...Qf6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/78d5a2d2-522a-41c7-b7a1-95f978783a78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We simply develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. This variation is extremely common and has been played in over 120 master games and 320,000 games, but it's a mistake because of 4.Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...Qf6 {4...Qf6 is by far the most common, but also the worst of the three options. A natural move to attack and defend. But as it turns out, after ...Qf6 Black is already borderline lost! We have the stunning idea - Nd5! We attack Black's queen and threaten to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7, but we also shockingly allow Black to capture on f2.} 5.Nd5 5...Qxf2+ {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture as every other move loses material - either to Nxc7+ or Qxg7. We slide our king over to d1, the only legal move. Now, our king is in no real danger, and we have multiple threats: Qxg7, Nxc7+ and even Nh3! Black will try to defend in two ways: 6...Kf8 and 6...g6.} 6.Kd1 6...Kf8 {Black defends the g7 pawn with the king and simultaneously moves out of the way of Nxc7+. However, Black is not out of the woods whatsoever. We attack the queen with Nh3! As it turns out, Black's queen doesn't really have any squares to move to other than d4, where it is going to get into a lot of trouble.} 7.Nh3 7...Qd4 {Black moves the queen to the only "safe" square and attacks the c4 bishop. We defend the bishop with d2-d3. Black's queen is suddenly starting to feel quite stuck, and our main threat would be c2-c3, which would trap Black's queen in the center of the board.} 8.d3 8...d6 {Black plays d7-d6 and opens up an attack on our queen. We simply move the queen to f3 - out of the vision of Black's bishop & still keeping the threat of c2-c3.} 9.Qf3 9...h5 {This is a very tricky move! Black wants to meet c2-c3 with Qxc4 and Bg4. We are still completely winning, but need to proceed with caution. We move the king once again! This move is a "cold shower" to Black as it simply refutes the whole idea of ...h5.} 10.Ke1 10...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop and attacks our queen.} 11.Qf1 {We simply slide the queen back to f1. Our threat of trapping Black's queen with c2-c3 is now impossible to prevent and White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 1: The Queen Defends (4...Qf6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/78d5a2d2-522a-41c7-b7a1-95f978783a78/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We simply develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. This variation is extremely common and has been played in over 120 master games and 320,000 games, but it's a mistake because of 4.Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...Qf6 {4...Qf6 is by far the most common, but also the worst of the three options. A natural move to attack and defend. But as it turns out, after ...Qf6 Black is already borderline lost! We have the stunning idea - Nd5! We attack Black's queen and threaten to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7, but we also shockingly allow Black to capture on f2.} 5.Nd5 5...Qxf2+ {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture as every other move loses material - either to Nxc7+ or Qxg7. We slide our king over to d1, the only legal move. Now, our king is in no real danger, and we have multiple threats: Qxg7, Nxc7+ and even Nh3! Black will try to defend in two ways: 6...Kf8 and 6...g6.} 6.Kd1 6...Kf8 {Black defends the g7 pawn with the king and simultaneously moves out of the way of Nxc7+. However, Black is not out of the woods whatsoever. We attack the queen with Nh3! As it turns out, Black's queen doesn't really have any squares to move to other than d4, where it is going to get into a lot of trouble.} 7.Nh3 7...h5 {This counterattack is actually Black's only hope of staying in the game. Our queen was attacked, so we slide it up to g5 - maintaining the attack on Black's queen.} 8.Qg5 8...Qd4 {Black moves the queen to the only safe square and attacks the c4 bishop. We defend the bishop with d2-d3.} 9.d3 9...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop and attacks our queen. This move also frees up the c5 square for Black's queen.} 10.Qg3 {We retreat our queen to g3. Now, it is highly unlikely that you will get this position in a game, but it is the critical variation that Black has to play not to be immediately lost. White is still better, the position is significantly more pleasant and easier to play for White, but with absolutely perfect play, Black might only be slightly worse.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Qf6 {Black defends f7 with the queen and offers a queen trade. Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7+.} 6.Nd5 6...Qxf3 {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture. We recapture on f3 with the knight. Black can defend against Nxc7+ in two ways: ...Bb6 & ...Bd6.} 7.Nxf3 7...Bb6 {...Bb6 is inferior, but considerably more common. We will play c2-c3. This move supports the b4 and d4 squares: One of our plans is to, at some point, take control of the center with a d4 push or to expand on the queenside with a4 + b4.} 8.c3 8...d6 {Black continues naturally with d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We go a2-a4. This move takes some space on the queenside and frees up the a2 square for our bishop.} 9.a4 9...Be6 {Black continues developing naturally with ...Be6.} 10.Ng5 {We play Ng5 and attack Black's bishop with the intention to capture it. White is now much better on all three parts of the board and Black doesn't really have any good moves. The engines already suggest that White is completely winning with a +2.5 advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Qf6 {Black defends f7 with the queen and offers a queen trade. Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to fork Black's king and rook with Nxc7+.} 6.Nd5 6...Qxf3 {Black is pretty much forced to make this capture. We recapture on f3 with the knight. Black can defend against Nxc7+ in two ways: ...Bb6 & ...Bd6.} 7.Nxf3 7...Bd6 {This is, objectively, the better option, although it cramps Black's position by blocking in the d-pawn and consequently makes it difficult for Black to develop the light-squared bishop.} 8.O-O {We simply castle our king. White is comfortably better here. Black has to somehow solve the problem of developing the c8 bishop, while in the meantime we will build up in the center with c3 + d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...O-O {Black castles their king before playing d7-d6. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop. 7.d3, and the most common response is 7...d6, which loses immediately to Bg5!} 7.d3 7...Nd4 {Black should play 7...Nd4, not 7...d6. This forces us to respond with Nxd4 as both our queen and the c2 pawn are threatened.} 8.Nxd4 8...exd4 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. 8...Bxd4 runs into 9.Bg5 and White wins a piece. We develop our bishop to g5 and attack Black's knight. Now capturing on c3 is not a great option since we would capture the f6 knight with our bishop and win full control of the dark squares on the kingside.} 9.Bg5 9...Be7 {Therefore, Black should defend the knight with ...Be7.} 10.Nb5 {We play Nb5 and attack the d4 pawn which really can't be conveniently defended. White has a pleasant advantage in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and attacks our queen. We are forced to slide the queen over to g3 as it is the only safe square.} 8.Qg3 8...Bxe2 {This is the most common move in the lichess database. Let's recapture.} 9.Nxe2 {Play can now develop in a handful of ways. The position is pretty comfortable and our general ideas include Qf3, developing the dark-squared bishop (to g5/h6), c3 and even O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and attacks our queen. We are forced to slide the queen over to g3 as it is the only safe square.} 8.Qg3 8...Qd7 {Black develops the queen, supports the g4 bishop and prepares O-O-O. Qh4 is a neat move! We use the fact that ...Qd7 undefended the f6 knight and immediately try to take advantage of that. Black should then move the knight and has two options: ...Nh5 & ...Ng8.} 9.Qh4 9...Nh5 {Black moves the knight to h5 - this is most common. Let's play f3. We attack the bishop and force it to move back to e6.} 10.f3 10...Be6 {Black retreats the bishop to e6.} 11.g4 {We attack the knight with g2-g4 and Black has a pretty hard time dealing with the attack. White is better here and the engine is already suggesting Black gives up a pawn with Nf4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and attacks our queen. We are forced to slide the queen over to g3 as it is the only safe square.} 8.Qg3 8...Qd7 {Black develops the queen, supports the g4 bishop and prepares O-O-O. Qh4 is a neat move! We use the fact that ...Qd7 undefended the f6 knight and immediately try to take advantage of that. Black should then move the knight and has two options: ...Nh5 & ...Ng8.} 9.Qh4 9...Ng8 {Passively retreating the knight to g8 is actually a better option for Black, though White should still be able to obtain a pleasant position.} 10.Bd2 {We develop the bishop to d2 and prepare O-O-O after which play can develop in a couple of different ways. Our general ideas include f3/f4, Nd5 and Bg5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and attacks our queen. We are forced to slide the queen over to g3 as it is the only safe square.} 8.Qg3 8...Be6 {Black retreats the bishop to e6 and offers a trade. We should trade the bishops on e6 and damage Black's structure.} 9.Bxe6 9...fxe6 {Black recaptures on e6. We develop our bishop to h6 - using the fact that the dark squares on the king-side were weakened with ...g6.} 10.Bh6 10...Qe7 {Black develops the queen and prepares O-O-O.} 11.Na4 {We play Na4 with the intention to, at some point, capture the bishop. The position is quite pleasant; we will castle queenside and play will develop from there.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 2: Black Defends With 4...g6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/596993fc-7eb5-4e4e-8ee0-bbc8c248fbab/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. This variation is known as the "Copycat variation" - since Black is mirroring/copying White's moves. Remember Qg4!} 4.Qg4 4...g6 {4...g6 is the second most common move. By playing g7-g6, Black has successfully defended the g-pawn but has also significantly weakened the dark squares on the kingside. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We will slide the queen back to f3 and line up an attack on the f7 square. Black has two ways to deal with that: 5...Nf6 and 5...Qf6.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our other knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play d2-d3 and prepare to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 7.d3 7...h6 {Black can also play ...h6 and prevent us from developing our bishop to g5 or h6.} 8.Bd2 {Instead we develop the bishop to d2 and prepare O-O-O. White now has a pleasant advantage due to the lead in development and Black's weakened kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 3: The King Defends (4...Kf8) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/05e753be-af5c-4699-acf4-88370adc2770/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. We now play 4.Qg4.} 4.Qg4 4...Kf8 {Black defends the g-pawn with the king - sacrificing the right to castle. This might seem somewhat risky at first glance, but we don't really have a way to immediately expose the king's placement. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We should play Qf3.} 5.Qf3 5...Qf6 {Black defends f7 with the queen and offers a queen trade - but this is actually inaccurate. Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6.Nd5 {Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6...Nd4 {Black can also counterattack our queen on f3. We should capture Black's queen on f6.} 7.Nxf6 7...Nxf3+ {Black captures our queen with check. We recapture on f3 with the knight.} 8.Nxf3 8...gxf6 {Black captures our knight with the g-pawn, since capturing with the knight would allow us to capture the e5-pawn.} 9.c3 {We play c2-c3 and prepare to expand in the center with d2-d4. White has a pleasant advantage here due to Black's ruined pawn structure (doubled f-pawns) and the slight lead in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 3: The King Defends (4...Kf8) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/05e753be-af5c-4699-acf4-88370adc2770/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. We now play 4.Qg4.} 4.Qg4 4...Kf8 {Black defends the g-pawn with the king - sacrificing the right to castle. This might seem somewhat risky at first glance, but we don't really have a way to immediately expose the king's placement. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We should play Qf3.} 5.Qf3 5...Qf6 {Black defends f7 with the queen and offers a queen trade - but this is actually inaccurate. Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6.Nd5 {Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6...Qxf3 {Black captures our queen on f3. We recapture on f3 with the knight. Black will then defend against Nxc7 in two ways: ...Bb6 & ...Bd6.} 7.Nxf3 7...Bb6 {...Bb6 is the most common way for Black to defend.} 8.a4 {We go a2-a4. This move takes some space on the queenside and frees up the a2 square for our bishop. White definitely has a pleasant advantage in this position and our general plan consists of d3 + c3 & even taking more queenside space with b4. Our plan can also consist of O-O, though it is very optional in this case since the king is actually pretty well positioned in the center of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 3: The King Defends (4...Kf8) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/05e753be-af5c-4699-acf4-88370adc2770/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. We now play 4.Qg4.} 4.Qg4 4...Kf8 {Black defends the g-pawn with the king - sacrificing the right to castle. This might seem somewhat risky at first glance, but we don't really have a way to immediately expose the king's placement. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We should play Qf3.} 5.Qf3 5...Qf6 {Black defends f7 with the queen and offers a queen trade - but this is actually inaccurate. Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6.Nd5 {Don't rush to trade queens! We play Nd5 - attacking Black's queen and threatening to capture the pawn on c7.} 6...Qxf3 {Black captures our queen on f3. We recapture on f3 with the knight. Black will then defend against Nxc7 in two ways: ...Bb6 & ...Bd6.} 7.Nxf3 7...Bd6 {This is also a decent way for Black to defend the c7-pawn, although it cramps Black's position by blocking in the d-pawn and consequently makes it difficult for them to develop the light-squared bishop.} 8.O-O {We simply castle our king. White has a very comfortable position here. Black has to somehow solve the problem of developing the c8 bishop, while in the meantime we will build up in the center with c3 + d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Vienna Game (Copycat Variation) / Study 3: The King Defends (4...Kf8) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/fd444435-7408-4987-80d4-2d5951e708f5/studies/05e753be-af5c-4699-acf4-88370adc2770/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We will play Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Bc5 {Black mirrors our move and naturally develops the bishop to c5. We now play 4.Qg4.} 4.Qg4 4...Kf8 {Black defends the g-pawn with the king - sacrificing the right to castle. This might seem somewhat risky at first glance, but we don't really have a way to immediately expose the king's placement. We must now notice that our queen on g4 is vulnerable and Black is threatening to win a piece with ...d5. We should play Qf3.} 5.Qf3 5...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and defends against the threat of Qxf7#. We proceed by developing our knight to e2, from where it supports the d4 square and prevents potential ...Nd4 ideas.} 6.Nge2 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play h2-h3, preventing Black's light-squared bishop from developing to the g4 square...} 7.h3 7...Be6 {So Black develops the bishop to e6.} 8.Bb3 {We slide the bishop back to b3 - inviting Black to trade on our terms and open the a-file for our rook. White is slightly better in this position and our general plan consists of d2-d3, developing the dark-squared bishop and castling our king.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Na5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/90d4c782-79d5-46c7-a0c1-5573eccff5ea/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Na5 {This is the critical move and Black's best attempt at equalizing! You won't face this often but it is important to know how to correctly respond. With ...Na5 Black is quickly exposing the downside of our move d2-d3. When we played d2-d3, we closed our c4 bishop's retreat route, and Black is quickly able to make use of that by attacking the bishop & grabbing the bishop pair. Let's bring our queen out to f3 to control d5.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc4 {Black justifies ...Na5 and captures the bishop, and we will recapture, leaving Black with a few plausible options for us to consider.} 6.dxc4 6...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop before playing d7-d6 and pins our c3 knight. We continue naturally by developing our knight to e2 and supporting the c3 knight.} 7.Ne2 7...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop.} 8.O-O {We simply castle our king. White has a minor edge in this position and we will continue to develop with b2-b3, Ng3 & Bg5. Also, most natural for Black is now to attack our queen with ...Bg4 after which we should simply move our queen to d3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Na5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/90d4c782-79d5-46c7-a0c1-5573eccff5ea/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Na5 {This is the critical move and Black's best attempt at equalizing! You won't face this often but it is important to know how to correctly respond. With ...Na5 Black is quickly exposing the downside of our move d2-d3. When we played d2-d3, we closed our c4 bishop's retreat route, and Black is quickly able to make use of that by attacking the bishop & grabbing the bishop pair. Let's bring our queen out to f3 to control d5.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc4 {Black justifies ...Na5 and captures the bishop, and we will recapture, leaving Black with a few plausible options for us to consider.} 6.dxc4 6...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop before playing d7-d6. We offer to exchange the dark-squared bishops.} 7.Be3 7...Bxe3 {Black exchanges on e3.} 8.Qxe3 {We recapture with the queen. There is no immediate danger for either side and the development is now pretty flexible. We will continue by developing our knight to e2 (from where it will generally go to g3), solidifying our queenside structure with b2-b3, and freely choosing where to castle our king.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Na5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/90d4c782-79d5-46c7-a0c1-5573eccff5ea/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Na5 {This is the critical move and Black's best attempt at equalizing! You won't face this often but it is important to know how to correctly respond. With ...Na5 Black is quickly exposing the downside of our move d2-d3. When we played d2-d3, we closed our c4 bishop's retreat route, and Black is quickly able to make use of that by attacking the bishop & grabbing the bishop pair. Let's bring our queen out to f3 to control d5.} 5.Qf3 5...c6 {This is the critical continuation - you will pretty much never face this. Black delays capturing on c4 and plays ...c6 - supporting further expansion with ...b5 or ...d5.} 6.Nge2 {We'll respond by developing our knight to e2. There are many ways for Black to approach this position, but our general plan is to develop with h3 + O-O and we can even play on the queenside with a2-a4. Also, we can meet the natural ...b5 with Bb3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Na5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/90d4c782-79d5-46c7-a0c1-5573eccff5ea/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Na5 {This is the critical move and Black's best attempt at equalizing! You won't face this often but it is important to know how to correctly respond. With ...Na5 Black is quickly exposing the downside of our move d2-d3. When we played d2-d3, we closed our c4 bishop's retreat route, and Black is quickly able to make use of that by attacking the bishop & grabbing the bishop pair. Let's bring our queen out to f3 to control d5.} 5.Qf3 5...Nxc4 {Black justifies ...Na5 and captures the bishop, and we will recapture, leaving Black with a few plausible options for us to consider.} 6.dxc4 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, supporting the e-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We play h2-h3, preventing Black's light-squared bishop from developing to the g4 square...} 7.h3 7...Be6 {So Black develops the bishop to e6 and attacks our c4-pawn. We defend the c4-pawn and solidify our queenside pawn structure.} 8.b3 8...Be7 {Black continues developing naturally. We develop our knight to the e2 square and prepare O-O.} 9.Nge2 9...c6 {Black continues natural development and prevents some Nd5 ideas that we might have in certain positions.} 10.O-O {This position can be reached from various move orders (Black can play O-O before c6 or Be7 before Be6 etc.). The setups and general middlegame ideas for White and Black are quite consistent. From an evaluation standpoint, White is slightly better in this position. Black is somewhat cramped, we have a great grip on the d5 square with our c and e pawns and will continue to develop as is highlighted on the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...O-O {Black can also O-O and choose to play the position without d7-d5. However, this gives White an edge. We castle ourselves as well. Black then has two options: ...d6 and ...h6.} 6.O-O 6...h6 {...h6 is the other option - Black is preventing us from developing with Bg5. We attack Black's b4 bishop - forcing a decision. Black has to decide whether to capture on c3 or move the bishop back to a5/c5.} 7.a3 7...Bxc3 {Capturing on c3 is actually the best option. We recapture on c3 with the knight.} 8.Nxc3 8...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop.} 9.f4 {We expand with f2-f4 and prepare a kingside attack. If allowed, we will also play f4-f5 to close the center and the diagonal. White has a comfortable edge in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...O-O {Black can also O-O and choose to play the position without d7-d5. However, this gives White an edge. We castle ourselves as well. Black then has two options: ...d6 and ...h6.} 6.O-O 6...h6 {...h6 is the other option - Black is preventing us from developing with Bg5. We attack Black's b4 bishop - forcing a decision. Black has to decide whether to capture on c3 or move the bishop back to a5/c5.} 7.a3 7...Ba5 {Most common is ...Ba5. We quickly strike at Black's center with f2-f4!} 8.f4 8...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn with d7-d6.} 9.f5 {This is important! We close the center with f4-f5 and prepare to attack Black on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...d5 {Black quickly strikes in the center with d7-d5. We capture the d5-pawn with our e-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the knight. We castle our king to remove the pin on our knight.} 7.O-O 7...Bxc3 {Black captures on c3 with the bishop. We recapture on c3 with the knight.} 8.Nxc3 8...Nxc3 {Black exchanges knights on c3 and damages our queenside pawn structure. We recapture on c3.} 9.bxc3 9...O-O {Black continues naturally with O-O.} 10.Qf3 {We develop our queen to f3. White is better and the computer suggests that Black's best attempt is to voluntarily double their e-pawns with Be6. Our attacking ideas are vast - we can expand on the queenside with a2-a4, our bishop can develop to a3 and we can put pressure on Black's e-pawn with Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...d5 {Black quickly strikes in the center with d7-d5. We capture the d5-pawn with our e-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the knight. We castle our king to remove the pin on our knight.} 7.O-O 7...Nxc3 {Black captures on c3 with the knight. We recapture with the b-pawn - damaging our structure but gaining an important tempo on Black's bishop.} 8.bxc3 8...Bd6 {Black retreats the bishop to d6. We move our knight to g3. From there, it has access to e4 and even f5 in some cases.} 9.Ng3 9...O-O {Black continues naturally with O-O.} 10.a4 {We expand on the queenside with a4. White has a slight edge and the position is certainly tricky for Black to play. We have various attacking ideas including Ne4, Qf3/Qh5 and Re1.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...O-O {Black can also O-O and choose to play the position without d7-d5. However, this gives White an edge. We castle ourselves as well. Black then has two options: ...d6 and ...h6.} 6.O-O 6...d6 {d7-d6 is most common - Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop.} 7.Bg5 {We develop our bishop to g5 and pin Black's knight. We will place our knight onto the d5 square and quickly attack Black with f2-f4. A natural move like ...h6 can now simply be met with Bxf6 + Nd5. White has a pleasant advantage in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...O-O {Black can also O-O and choose to play the position without d7-d5. However, this gives White an edge. We castle ourselves as well. Black then has two options: ...d6 and ...h6.} 6.O-O 6...h6 {...h6 is the other option - Black is preventing us from developing with Bg5. We attack Black's b4 bishop - forcing a decision. Black has to decide whether to capture on c3 or move the bishop back to a5/c5.} 7.a3 7...Bc5 {Black can also move the bishop to c5 - temporarily preventing f2-f4 by pinning our f2 pawn. We attack the bishop with b2-b4! If the bishop moves off of the g1-a7 diagonal, we will play f2-f4.} 8.b4 8...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 - maintaining the pin.} 9.Na4 {We jump our knight out to a4 with the intention to capture Black's bishop - not only will this win us the bishop pair, but it will also finally allow us to play our desired f2-f4. White has easier play and a comfortable advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...d5 {Black quickly strikes in the center with d7-d5. We capture the d5-pawn with our e-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the knight. We castle our king to remove the pin on our knight.} 7.O-O 7...Be6 {Black develops with the c8 bishop to e6 and defends the d5 knight. We attack Black's b4 bishop - forcing a decision. Black now has to decide whether to capture on c3 or retreat the bishop.} 8.a3 8...Bxc3 {Black decides to capture on c3 - this is most common. We are actually forced to recapture with the b-pawn since capturing with the knight would lead to a tripling of the c-pawns.} 9.bxc3 9...O-O {Black continues natural development with O-O.} 10.a4 {We expand on the queenside with a3-a4. If possible, we would also like to push the pawn to a5. We have two bishops and an open b-file so we can try to use it. Our dark-squared bishop can develop flexibly (b2/d2/a3). The position is objectively still equal though.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bb4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/40cb9174-6817-4d73-b72e-e37dbe1a52dd/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bb4 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and pins our c3 knight. The main idea of this move is that Black is trying to "undermine" our central control and quickly strike with d7-d5. We develop our knight to e2 and support the c3 knight. This move also supports a future f2-f4 and the knight will often go to g3.} 5.Ne2 5...d5 {Black quickly strikes in the center with d7-d5. We capture the d5-pawn with our e-pawn.} 6.exd5 6...Nxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 with the knight. We castle our king to remove the pin on our knight.} 7.O-O 7...Be6 {Black develops with the c8 bishop to e6 and defends the d5 knight. We attack Black's b4 bishop - forcing a decision. Black now has to decide whether to capture on c3 or retreat the bishop.} 8.a3 8...Be7 {Black decides not to give up the bishop on c3 and instead retreats it to e7. We capture on d5 with the bishop.} 9.Bxd5 9...Bxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We strike with f2-f4. Black definitely shouldn't capture on f4 since Nxf4 (followed by capturing the light-squared bishop) would be devastating.} 10.f4 10...O-O {Instead, Black ignores f4 and castles their king. We push f4-f5 - closing the center and preparing a kingside attack.} 11.f5 11...Nd4 {Black puts their knight on d4 and offers an exchange with the intention to simplify the position. This position has been reached in three master games, but we have a very interesting novelty prepared... f6! This move might seem completely absurd at first but it has a concrete idea - we want to open up Black's king.} 12.f6 12...Bxf6 {Black recaptures on f6 with the bishop. We now play Rxf6! A brilliant exchange sacrifice! We are practically forcing Black to capture with the g-pawn and open their king since Qxf6 would allow us to capture the d5 bishop.} 13.Rxf6 13...gxf6 {Black recaptures with the g-pawn as to not lose material. We capture the d4 knight and simultaneously open the d1-h5 diagonal for our queen.} 14.Nxd4 14...exd4 {Black recaptures on d4. We should not rush to give a check with our queen, but instead we should play Bh6 first. We are attacking Black's rook and threatening checkmate in 2 moves with Qg4+ & Qg7#.} 15.Bh6 15...Kh8 {Black moves the king to h8 and defends against the checkmate threat. We capture Black's light-squared bishop.} 16.Nxd5 16...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5.} 17.Qf1 {We move our queen to f1 and threaten checkmate with Qxf6+ & Qg7#. Black must defend against the threat of checkmate, after which we will capture the f8 rook and enter an objectively equal endgame. Down a pawn for the moment, but with Black's ruined structure and weak king, White should have good compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bc5 and 6...Bg4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/1c2fb1eb-e6eb-41fc-b898-3d78dc154158/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is the most common move, and it allows us to get our desired setup. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Most common is ...Bg4. Black develops the bishop and pins our f3 knight - reducing our control over the d4 square. Keep Na4 in mind! This is our best way of dealing with ...Bg4 and the threat of ...Nd4. Our move Na4 attacks Black's strong dark-squared bishop and frees up the c3 square so that we can prevent/meet Nd4 with c2-c3.} 7.Na4 7...Bb6 {Black shouldn't allow us to capture on c5 and irreparably damage their pawn structure, so their best option is to retreat the bishop to b6. We shouldn't rush to capture on b6 - the bishop isn't going anywhere. Instead, we throw in h2-h3 and force a decision from Black's light-squared bishop.} 8.h3 8...Bxf3 {By far most common and most logical is ...Bxf3. Black gives up the bishop pair which was going to be lost anyway and instead gains control over the d4 square. We don't want to ruin our structure with doubled f-pawns so we recapture on f3 with the queen.} 9.Qxf3 9...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight to d4 - attacking our queen & the c2-pawn. We retreat the queen to f2 - defending the c-pawn. It might feel counterintuitive to put the queen in direct line of a potential discovered attack from the b6 bishop, but Black simply doesn't have any useful discoveries, and our intention is to capture that bishop on the next move.} 10.Qf2 10...Ba5+ {Black's only attempt at saving the bishop is to give a check on a5. The line that follows is very forced: we block the check with c2-c3.} 11.c3 11...b5 {Black forks our a4 knight and c4 bishop with b7-b5. We unpin our c-pawn by castling the king and are now ready to capture Black's d4 knight.} 12.O-O 12...bxc4 {Black captures the c4 bishop.} 13.cxd4 {We capture the knight on d4. White is better here. We're castled and ready to shred open the center - Black can't even castle and try to safeguard their king since we would just capture the e-pawn and establish a dominant pawn center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bc5 and 6...Bg4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/1c2fb1eb-e6eb-41fc-b898-3d78dc154158/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is the most common move, and it allows us to get our desired setup. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Most common is ...Bg4. Black develops the bishop and pins our f3 knight - reducing our control over the d4 square. Keep Na4 in mind! This is our best way of dealing with ...Bg4 and the threat of ...Nd4. Our move Na4 attacks Black's strong dark-squared bishop and frees up the c3 square so that we can prevent/meet Nd4 with c2-c3.} 7.Na4 7...Nd4 {Black can also try to play ...Nd4 immediately, but this isn't considered to be any good. We attack the knight with c2-c3.} 8.c3 8...Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3.} 9.gxf3 {We recapture with the f-pawn - gaining a tempo on Black's light-squared bishop. Black has to move the bishop and we will follow up by capturing the dark-squared bishop on c5 - completely ruining Black's pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bc5 and 6...Bg4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/1c2fb1eb-e6eb-41fc-b898-3d78dc154158/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is the most common move, and it allows us to get our desired setup. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Most common is ...Bg4. Black develops the bishop and pins our f3 knight - reducing our control over the d4 square. Keep Na4 in mind! This is our best way of dealing with ...Bg4 and the threat of ...Nd4. Our move Na4 attacks Black's strong dark-squared bishop and frees up the c3 square so that we can prevent/meet Nd4 with c2-c3.} 7.Na4 7...O-O {Black could simply castle, ignoring the attack on their bishop. Their idea is that they'll recapture with the c-pawn and gain a semi-open d-file. As always, we'll execute our knight for bishop trade because we want to clear the way for 0-0.} 8.Nxc5 8...dxc5 {Black recaptures. ...Nd4 is an idea. But we can just castle.} 9.O-O 9...Nd4 {The idea is that after ...Nd4, we can kick that knight out. c3 attacks the knight.} 10.c3 10...Nxf3+ {If Black takes, we can recapture.} 11.gxf3 11...Bh3 {The bishop attacks our rook, and we remove it from danger.} 12.Rf2 12...exf4 {And if Black persists by continuing to go for exchanges, we capture the pawn on f4 back. Our center is quite overwhelming.} 13.Bxf4 13...Nh5 {If your bishop is attacked, e3 is the best square for it.} 14.Be3 14...Qh4 {If the queen comes into h4.} 15.Qd2 {We've got strong ideas of Bxc5, Kh1, and the engine's best move for Black is the unintuitive ...Be6!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...h6 {Black can also play prophylactically by preventing Bg5 with h7-h6. We will continue with Nd5, offering a trade of knights.} 8.Nd5 8...Na5 {Black can also decline the trade and continue by attacking our bishop with ...Na5. We capture the knight on f6 with check.} 9.Nxf6+ 9...Qxf6 {Black recaptures with the queen to keep the kingside pawn structure intact.} 10.g4 {We start a kingside attack with g2-g4! Our general plan is now to continue with Qe2 + Be3 + O-O-O and even a well-timed g4-g5. White has a very pleasant position and a brewing attack which is, practically, very difficult to defend against.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {Black jumps the knight to g4 and threatens a fork on f2. At first glance, it might seem as though White doesn't really have a good defense against the threat of Nf2, but unbeknownst to Black, we have a really nice response prepared. We counterattack Black's f7 pawn with Ng5!} 7.Ng5 7...O-O {By far most common is ...O-O - safeguarding the king and keeping the threat of Nf2 alive. We continue f4-f5! We've now closed the h3-c8 diagonal and are threatening to capture Black's undefended g4 knight.} 8.f5 8...Bf2+ {Black can also give this check on f2 and force some exchanges. This move is about as common as ...Nf6. We move the king to f1.} 9.Kf1 9...Ne3+ {Black plays Ne3+ - forking our king & queen. We are forced to capture the knight since we don't want to lose our queen.} 10.Bxe3 10...Bxe3 {Black recaptures on e3 and attacks our knight. We defend the knight with h2-h4! This move is handy since we will also be able to open the h-file for our rook upon recapturing.} 11.h4 11...Bxg5 {Black captures the knight on g5. We recapture on g5 and open the h- file. The g-pawn is now en prise.} 12.hxg5 12...Qxg5 {Black grabs the pawn on g5. We move the rook to h5 and attack Black's queen.} 13.Rh5 13...Qf4+ {Black moves the queen to f4 and gives a check.} 14.Kg1 {Make sure to move the king to g1 - we don't really want to be offering any queen trades with moves like Qf3, since we are currently down a pawn. According to the computer, White has a winning advantage, but this advantage can be tricky to prove in practical play. The computer evaluates this position as +2.5 for White, but in Frank, Mario Alberto - Krol, Wladyslaw (2001.), White didn't manage to convert his advantage and the game ended in a draw.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...Na5 {...Na5 is the most common move. Black attacks the c4 bishop and tries to quickly grab the bishop pair. We ignore Black's threat and pin the f6 knight. In a perfect world, we will place our knight on the d5 square and capture Black's f6 knight - completely ruining Black's kingside pawn structure.} 8.Bg5 8...Nxc4 {Black justifies ...Na5 and captures the bishop. We recapture on c4.} 9.dxc4 9...c6 {This is by far the most common idea. Black plays c7-c6 and covers the d5 square with the c-pawn.} 10.Qe2 {We develop the queen to e2 and prepare O-O-O. We have a pleasant advantage and our game plan is to attack Black on the kingside with our g and h pawns. Most games now continue h6 Bxf6, after which we will be able to combine O-O-O, g2-g4 and h2-h4 to start a crushing attack on Black's kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...Na5 {...Na5 is the most common move. Black attacks the c4 bishop and tries to quickly grab the bishop pair. We ignore Black's threat and pin the f6 knight. In a perfect world, we will place our knight on the d5 square and capture Black's f6 knight - completely ruining Black's kingside pawn structure.} 8.Bg5 8...c6 {Black can also continue ...c6 immediately - this is slightly more accurate and I will explain why at the end of the line. We play a2-a3, threatening to "save" our light-squared bishop by retreating it to a2.} 9.a3 9...Nxc4 {Black captures on c4 as to not allow us to keep the bishop.} 10.dxc4 {We recapture on c4 and will continue to develop with Qe2 and O-O-O. The reason that this move order is actually a better option for Black is simply because we got the same position as in the main continuation, just that we used an extra tempo on a2-a3. This means all of our typical ideas will come a move "slower" and Black has a little bit more time to try to consolidate. However, our position is still much easier to play and we have a comfortable objective edge.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...Nd4 {This is a close second in popularity to the main move ...Na5. We don't simply capture on d4, but instead respond with Nd5!} 8.Nd5 8...Nxd5 {Black captures our knight on d5. We recapture on d5.} 9.Bxd5 9...c6 {Black attacks our bishop with c7-c6. We retreat the bishop to b3, allowing Black to capture our bishop and grab the bishop pair.} 10.Bb3 10...Nxb3 {Black captures the bishop on b3.} 11.axb3 {We recapture with the a-pawn. The position is very rich & dynamic and, objectively, White has a slight edge. We will develop our queen to e2 and our dark-squared bishop can develop to d2 or offer to trade itself on e3. We also have great attacking potential with g2-g4 and h2-h4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...h6 {Black can also play prophylactically by preventing Bg5 with h7-h6. We will continue with Nd5, offering a trade of knights.} 8.Nd5 8...Nxd5 {Black captures our knight on d5.} 9.Bxd5 {We recapture on d5. White has a comfortable advantage and our general development plan is now Qe2 + Bd2 + O-O-O. Any attack on our bishop can comfortably be met with Bb3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {...O-O is second most common behind ...Bg4. Black doesn't initiate any immediate battles and instead quickly safeguards the king. f5 is a common idea in these positions.} 7.f5 7...Na5 {...Na5 is the most common move. Black attacks the c4 bishop and tries to quickly grab the bishop pair. We ignore Black's threat and pin the f6 knight. In a perfect world, we will place our knight on the d5 square and capture Black's f6 knight - completely ruining Black's kingside pawn structure.} 8.Bg5 8...Nxc4 {Black justifies ...Na5 and captures the bishop. We recapture on c4.} 9.dxc4 9...Bb4 {This is critical, but considerably rarer than ...c6. Black pins the c3 knight, thereby preventing Nd5. Nd2! We can now play this completely absurd computer suggested novelty! We allow Black to triple our c-pawns, but quickly begin a three move reroute of the f3 knight, which wasn't really playing a major role.} 10.Nd2 10...Bxc3 {Black captures on c3 and forces us to triple the c-pawns.} 11.bxc3 {We recapture on c3. Our main idea is now to reroute the knight through f1 to e3 or g3. Black is cramped and has troubles getting their other pieces into play, while we still have the standard attacking ideas with g2-g4 and h2-h4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {Black jumps the knight to g4 and threatens a fork on f2. At first glance, it might seem as though White doesn't really have a good defense against the threat of Nf2, but unbeknownst to Black, we have a really nice response prepared. We counterattack Black's f7 pawn with Ng5!} 7.Ng5 7...O-O {By far most common is ...O-O - safeguarding the king and keeping the threat of Nf2 alive. We continue f4-f5! We've now closed the h3-c8 diagonal and are threatening to capture Black's undefended g4 knight.} 8.f5 8...Nf6 {...Nf6 is not the most common, but is the most resilient.} 9.Nd5 {We put our knight on d5 with the intention to trade it off on f6, after which we will continue our attack with g4 and h4. White has a lot of easy play and a very pleasant advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {Black jumps the knight to g4 and threatens a fork on f2. At first glance, it might seem as though White doesn't really have a good defense against the threat of Nf2, but unbeknownst to Black, we have a really nice response prepared. We counterattack Black's f7 pawn with Ng5!} 7.Ng5 7...O-O {By far most common is ...O-O - safeguarding the king and keeping the threat of Nf2 alive. We continue f4-f5! We've now closed the h3-c8 diagonal and are threatening to capture Black's undefended g4 knight.} 8.f5 8...Nf2 {This is the most common move, but a massive blunder!} 9.Qh5 {We play Qh5! Black cannot defend against the threats on the f7 & h7 pawns. White is now completely winning - the computer is even suggesting that Black is so lost, that their best option is to give up the queen with ...Qxg5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {Black jumps the knight to g4 and threatens a fork on f2. At first glance, it might seem as though White doesn't really have a good defense against the threat of Nf2, but unbeknownst to Black, we have a really nice response prepared. We counterattack Black's f7 pawn with Ng5!} 7.Ng5 7...h6 {Black attacks our knight with h7-h6. This move might not make much sense at first glance, but we actually shouldn't rush to capture the f7-pawn, since it would allow an annoying Qh4+. Instead, we should continue f4-f5.} 8.f5 8...hxg5 {Black captures the knight on g5. We capture Black's knight on g4.} 9.Qxg4 9...f6 {Black solidifies the kingside pawn structure and supports the g-pawn with f7-f6. We play h2-h4. In a perfect world, we will be able to open the h-file and quickly get to Black's king.} 10.h4 10...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4 and threatens Nxc2+, forking our king and rook. However, Black is certainly not ready for our next move. We completely ignore Black's threat and play Rh3! The idea is that the rook on h3 is defended by the queen, and we want to shred open the h-file to get to Black's king.} 11.Rh3 11...Nxc2+ {Black continues with their plan and plays Nxc2+. We move the king to d1 and attack Black's knight.} 12.Kd1 12...Nxa1 {Black captures the a1 rook. We capture on g5 and open the h-file. This is where Black's issues become apparent.} 13.hxg5 13...Rxh3 {Black captures our rook on h3. We recapture on h3 with the queen. The devastating threat is now Qh8+.} 14.Qxh3 14...Kd7 {Black runs the king to d7!} 15.Qh7 {We move our queen to h7 and attack the g-pawn. Black is incredibly underdeveloped and will simply not be able to deal with our attack. White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 4: 4...Bc5 Sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/cfa4f544-7bfb-4f01-bdb0-c58ec0044b57/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...d6 {Black supports the e-pawn and prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {Black jumps the knight to g4 and threatens a fork on f2. At first glance, it might seem as though White doesn't really have a good defense against the threat of Nf2, but unbeknownst to Black, we have a really nice response prepared. We counterattack Black's f7 pawn with Ng5!} 7.Ng5 7...Nf2 {The immediate ...Nf2 might look attractive, but it is a massive mistake! We play Qh5! - threatening checkmate on f7.} 8.Qh5 8...g6 {Black defends against the mate threat by moving the g-pawn and attacking our queen.} 9.Bxf7+ {We throw in Bxf7+! Black's king is now forced to move and simply won't be able to survive the oncoming attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 5: Sidelines After 4...Bc5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/160516cd-7c2f-438e-8e5b-1465993461a9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black captures the f4-pawn. It might seem as though this move only helps us develop, but Black actually has some very concrete ideas. We recapture on f4 and develop our c1 bishop.} 6.Bxf4 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. Let's play Nf3 to develop.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black continues naturally and safeguards the king with O-O. We play d3-d4! This move is pretty rare, but it is the top choice of the engine.} 8.d4 8...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6, keeping pressure on our d-pawn which can quickly come under fire. We continue with Bg5. Black was actually threatening a typical tactical idea with ...Nxe4 Nxe4 d5!, so we opt to pin the f6 knight in order to prevent said idea.} 9.Bg5 9...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and pins our f3-knight. Black's main idea is to capture the knight and win the d-pawn. We continue Qd2 - preparing to castle queenside.} 10.Qd2 10...Bxf3 {Black continues with their plan and captures on f3. We recapture on f3.} 11.gxf3 11...Nxd4 {Black captures the d-pawn. We slide our queen over to g2 and are ready to start a kingside attack.} 12.Qg2 12...Ne6 {Black retreats the knight to e6 and attacks our g5 bishop.} 13.h4 {We support the bishop with h2-h4. Black now shouldn't capture the g5 bishop as the opening of the h-file would lead to a crushing attack. Down a pawn for the moment, but with rich attacking ideas and great dynamic compensation. The position is objectively equal, but requires very accurate defense from Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 5: Sidelines After 4...Bc5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/160516cd-7c2f-438e-8e5b-1465993461a9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black captures the f4-pawn. It might seem as though this move only helps us develop, but Black actually has some very concrete ideas. We recapture on f4 and develop our c1 bishop.} 6.Bxf4 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. Let's play Nf3 to develop.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black continues naturally and safeguards the king with O-O. We play d3-d4! This move is pretty rare, but it is the top choice of the engine.} 8.d4 8...Bb4 {Black responds Bb4, pinning our c3 knight and thereby threatening to capture the e-pawn.} 9.Bg5 {We respond with Bg5, pinning Black's knight! Our development plan is now O-O + Qd3. We will also capture Black's knight when our g5 bishop is provoked with a move like h7-h6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 5: Sidelines After 4...Bc5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/160516cd-7c2f-438e-8e5b-1465993461a9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...O-O {Black immediately safeguards the king with O-O. We continue with our plan and develop the g1 knight to f3. Now the main moves 6...d6 and 6...exf4 transpose.} 6.Nf3 6...Ng4 {...Ng4! Black is eyeing the f2 square with their pieces and our typical response Ng5 isn't as effective since Black has already castled and is now ready to meet Ng5 with d7-d5! Instead we simply move our queen and avoid the knight fork on f2. If ...Nf2, we play Rf1.} 7.Qe2 7...Bf2+ {Instead, Black plays Bf2+ and forces our king to move. We slide the king over to f1.} 8.Kf1 8...Nd4 {Black jumps their other knight into d4 and attacks our queen. We capture the d4 knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Bxd4 {Black recaptures on d4 with their bishop. We continue h2-h3 and force Black's knight to move.} 10.h3 10...Nf2 {Black continues with Nf2 and attacks our rook.} 11.Rg1 {We move the rook to g1. Ideally, we want to continue by trading Black's bishop with Be3. This position is still objectively equal, but Black's only good move is the pawn sacrifice with d7-d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 5: Sidelines After 4...Bc5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/160516cd-7c2f-438e-8e5b-1465993461a9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black captures the f4-pawn. It might seem as though this move only helps us develop, but Black actually has some very concrete ideas. We recapture on f4 and develop our c1 bishop.} 6.Bxf4 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. Let's play Nf3 to develop.} 7.Nf3 7...Be6 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to e6 and offers a trade. We decline the trade and move the bishop to b5. We want to ruin Black's structure by capturing on c6, and develop with Qd2 + O-O-O.} 8.Bb5 8...a6 {Black attacks our bishop and forces a decision. We continue with our plan by capturing on c6 and ruining Black's pawn structure.} 9.Bxc6+ 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 10.Qd2 {We continue Qd2 and prepare O-O-O. White has a slight advantage and we will continue to develop with O-O-O + Rhe1. The middlegame ideas are rich, but one of our main game plans is to play in the center by preparing an eventual e4-e5 or d3-d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Vienna Game (3 Knights Variation) / Study 5: Sidelines After 4...Bc5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/775a4eea-5a50-47da-b7dd-5790ef829fbe/chapters/5199b52a-f548-49e6-8b65-2200db856e70/studies/160516cd-7c2f-438e-8e5b-1465993461a9/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies with the mirror image e5, the most popular reply at all levels. We develop our queenside knight in order to preserve the f2-f4 push.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {In this chapter, Black replies with Nc6. We will develop the light-squared bishop to c4. In a perfect world, we'll play d3, f4, Nf3, and castle.} 3.Bc4 3...Nf6 {Black develops their other knight naturally on f6 and puts some pressure on our e-pawn. We play d2-d3.} 4.d3 4...Bc5 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop naturally on the c5 square. This is most common and allows us to get our desired set-up. We expand with f2-f4 and look to develop our knight behind the f-pawn.} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black captures the f4-pawn. It might seem as though this move only helps us develop, but Black actually has some very concrete ideas. We recapture on f4 and develop our c1 bishop.} 6.Bxf4 6...d6 {Black plays d7-d6, preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. Let's play Nf3 to develop.} 7.Nf3 7...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and pins our f3 knight.} 8.Qd2 {We simply move out of the pin and prepare O-O-O. The position is pretty comfortable and some typical middlegame ideas include Bg5 and Nd5. In many variations, one of our rooks will also move to the f-file in order to meet an eventual ...Nd4 from Black.} *